WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.484
FP32 uses 32-bit floating-point values and has been a common training and reference precision.

00:00:06.484 --> 00:00:13.387
FP16 uses half as many bits, reducing memory and taking advantage of supported accelerator operations.

00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:23.636
BF16 also uses 16 bits but allocates them differently, preserving a broad numerical range that is useful in training and some inference workflows.

00:00:23.636 --> 00:00:28.866
Hardware support determines whether a precision provides an actual speed benefit.

00:00:28.866 --> 00:00:35.768
INT8 represents quantized values with 8-bit integers, often with per-tensor, per-channel, or grouped scale information.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:46.018
Four-bit and lower-bit weight formats reduce storage further, but the nominal bit count does not include every scale, lookup, alignment, or metadata cost.

00:00:46.018 --> 00:00:52.502
Actual file and loaded sizes are therefore larger than parameters multiplied by bits alone.

00:00:52.502 --> 00:00:55.640
Weights are the learned model parameters.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.451
Activations are intermediate values produced while the model processes input.

00:01:00.451 --> 00:01:09.445
A system may store weights at low precision while computing activations or selected layers at higher precision to preserve quality.

00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:17.603
The key-value cache has its own format and memory cost, which grows with context length and active sequences.

00:01:17.603 --> 00:01:20.322
Precision affects more than size.

00:01:20.322 --> 00:01:25.969
It changes numerical resolution, supported kernels, memory traffic, and sometimes output behavior.

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:32.454
Lower is not automatically faster if the hardware or runtime lacks an efficient implementation.

00:01:32.454 --> 00:01:42.285
Treat labels such as 4-bit as a starting description, then inspect the quantization method, runtime support, measured memory, speed, and task quality.
